1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:26,000
CampusMarket — a campus second-hand trading platform (Track 1 · Database Systems)

2
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Live metrics from SQLite: 1,000 users · 5 categories · 10,000 products (3,961 available)

3
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Marketplace — every query is parameterized SQL executed in the repository layer

4
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Keyword search — 140 available items match 'mouse' (composite index seek)

5
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Browsing the matching results

6
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:37,000
Price-range filter (10–800 CNY) combined with the keyword

7
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:47,000
Sort: price highest first — ORDER BY expressions come from a fixed whitelist

8
00:01:47,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Pagination via LIMIT/OFFSET — page 2 of 2; COUNT(*) computes the page count

9
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:22,000
All filters applied in one parameterized query — 30 items across 2 pages

10
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:37,000
Seller management — publish, edit, and delete your own products (account #1)

11
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:52,000
Publishing is validated by the service layer: title length, valid category, price > 0

12
00:02:52,000 --> 00:03:05,000
Filling in the listing: DEMO Used Monitor 1 · ¥39.50 · Electronics

13
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:20,000
Published: one INSERT into products — the listing goes live with status 'available'

14
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:35,000
Product #10002 appears at the top of My Products (17 items)

15
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:48,000
The edit form is ownership-checked — UPDATE … WHERE product_id = ? AND seller_id = ?

16
00:03:48,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Sold rows keep buyer & finished_at and cannot be deleted

17
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,000
Switching to buyer account #2 via the sidebar selector

18
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:17,000
Browsing the marketplace as the buyer — 3,962 available items (the new listing is live)

19
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:29,000
Searching for the demo listing by its unique title

20
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Product detail — seller #1, category Electronics, price ¥39.50, status available

21
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:57,000
Purchase = one conditional UPDATE … WHERE status='available' — exactly-once, decided by rowcount

22
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:10,000
Redirected to My Purchases — purchase #10002 tops the history (7 items, ¥293.52)

23
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:22,000
Opening the product detail again from the purchase history

24
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:35,000
Revisiting the item: status 'sold', buyer #2, finished_at 2026-11-22 00:56:01

25
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:47,000
A second purchase is rejected — the banner confirms the item is already sold

26
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:56,000
The transaction record lives on the product row — buyer, seller and finished_at

27
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:05,000
Back to the home page — metrics update live from the database

28
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:19,000
13 automated tests pass: purchase semantics, 10 concurrent races, integrity, user journey

29
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:31,000
Concurrency evidence: 10 rounds — exactly 1 winner and 1 loser per round

30
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:42,000
Database internals: three tables, two indexes, zero foreign-key violations

31
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:51,000
Three-layer architecture: Streamlit → services → repositories → connection factory → SQLite

32
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:59,000
ER model: users publish & purchase products; categories group them

33
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:10,000
Exactly-once purchases · layered design · verified by tests — thank you for watching

